    pr_ref:  # a3f8
    description: "**Minimal repro** (analyzer called directly, no pipeline):\n\n    class A { fun f(): Int = 1 }\n    class B { fun g(): Int = 2 }\n\n    analyze_kotlin(...)  ->  [('f', 'function')]\n\nBoth classes vanish. `A.f` is re-emitted as a TOP-LEVEL FUNCTION named `f`\n(wrong kind, wrong name, no owner). `B` and `B.g` vanish entirely. No exception,\nno warning, no `ERROR` diagnostic — the analyzer returns a well-formed, wrong\nresult.\n\n**Narrowed by bisection.** The trigger is multiplicity of *bodied* type\ndeclarations, nothing else:\n\n    class A { fun f(): Int = 1 }                    -> [('A','class'), ('A.f','method')]   OK\n    class A { fun f(): Int { return 1 } }           -> [('A','class'), ('A.f','method')]   OK\n    class A\\nclass B                                -> [('A','class'), ('B','class')]      OK\n    class A { }\\nclass B { }                        -> [('A','class'), ('B','class')]      OK\n    class A { fun f(): Int = 1 }\\nclass B {...}     -> [('f','function')]                  BROKEN\n    class A { fun f(): Int { return 1 } }\\nclass B  -> [('f','function')]                  BROKEN\n    class A { fun f() { } }\\nclass B { fun g() { } }-> [('f','function')]                  BROKEN\n    3 classes with bodies                           -> [('f','function')]                  BROKEN\n\nBody style is irrelevant (expression-body, block-body, and no-return-type all\nfail identically). Empty bodies are fine. ONE bodied declaration is fine.\n\n**It is NOT the parse.** tree-sitter produces the correct tree in both cases:\n\n    TWO ->  source_file\n              class_declaration ( class, type_identifier, class_body )\n              class_declaration ( class, type_identifier, class_body )\n\nBoth `class_declaration` nodes are present and well-formed. The defect is in the\nanalyzer's traversal, which dispatches on `node.type == \"class_declaration\"`\n(kotlin.py:822). Note `root_node.has_error` is True for EVERY case above,\nincluding the ones that work — so has_error is a red herring, not the trigger,\nand any fix must not key on it.\n\n**Verified pre-existing, not a WI-dorop regression.** Reproduces byte-identically\nat `9f0a163833^` (the parent of the WI-dorop enum-member commit), checked by\nrestoring that file version and re-running, not inferred.\n\n**Each construct is correct in ISOLATION**, which is why no existing test caught\nit — the unit tests almost certainly exercise one declaration at a time:\n\n    class Square { fun area(): Int = 1 }      -> ('Square','class') + ('Square.area','method')\n    interface Shape { fun area(): Int }       -> ('Shape','interface') + ('Shape.area','method')\n    abstract class Base { abstract fun ... }  -> ('Base','class', modifiers=['abstract'])\n    enum class Color { RED, GREEN }           -> ('Color','class', ['enum']) + 2 fields\n\nThat last two lines are worth noting: Kotlin's `abstract` modifier emission is\nBETTER than typescript's or cpp's (which emit no modifier at all). The analyzer\nis not weak; it is broken on a condition every realistic multi-type file meets.\n\n**Scope NOT measured, deliberately.** I have not measured what fraction of real\nKotlin files carry 2+ bodied type declarations, so I am not claiming \"the Kotlin\nanalyzer is non-functional.\" What is established: any such file loses every type\nsymbol and gains one misclassified function. A single-class file is unaffected.\nQuantifying this on a real Kotlin corpus is the first step of the fix.\n\n**How it was found.** Incidentally, during the type-kind concept audit — Kotlin\nwas one of 11 languages in a cross-language abstract-vs-concrete probe and was\nthe only one that emitted NOTHING. First reaction was \"missing grammar\"; the\ngrammar is present and parses correctly (L10 — suspect the measurement first,\nthen keep going when the measurement holds up).\n\n**Consequence.** Silent and total for affected files: no class/interface nodes,\nso no containment, no inheritance edges, no dispatch, no dead-code reachability,\nand a phantom top-level function in the call graph. Every downstream consumer\nsees a Kotlin file that appears to contain one loose function."  # a3f8
